<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="sso"
      toc={[
        {"sso-login", "Setting up SSO login"},
        {"okta", "Okta setup"},
        {"jumpcloud", "JumpCloud setup"},
        {"keycloak", "Keycloak setup"},
        {"security-notes", "Security notes"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="sso">
        <:dek>
          On Team and Enterprise plans, your team signs in to emisar with your own identity
          provider — Google Workspace, Okta, Keycloak, or any compliant OIDC.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        This page sets up <strong class="text-zinc-200">SSO login</strong>
        — signing in through your IdP. Provisioning and deprovisioning from the directory is a
        separate piece that builds on a configured connection: <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim"}
          class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
        >directory sync (SCIM)</.link>.
        Both are managed from <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team → Single sign-on</strong>
        in your emisar workspace — the Team page lists your connections, and each connection's own
        page holds its configuration.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="Where this flow stands">
        <p>
          emisar's OIDC authorization-code and just-in-time provisioning flow is verified end to
          end against Keycloak 26.7, and the Okta steps below were walked on a live Okta org —
          every screenshot is that console. JumpCloud tenant certification is still in progress.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_h2 id="sso-login">Setting up SSO login</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        You need an emisar owner or admin. Open
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team → Single sign-on</strong>
        and click <strong class="text-zinc-200">Add provider</strong>; the form opens on its own
        page and walks you through the provider-side app as you pick a provider type.
        <.plan_note tier={:team} />
      </p>

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-6">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Create an OAuth / OIDC app at your provider.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              A confidential web application with a client secret. emisar uses standard,
              discovery-based OpenID Connect, so the named providers (Google Workspace, Okta,
              Keycloak) are presets over one flow — there's no separate integration to wait for, and
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Generic OpenID Connect</strong>
              fits anything compliant. When you pick a provider type in the form, the in-app guide
              names the exact admin-console path for it. Leave
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">DPoP</strong>
              (sender-constrained tokens) off — emisar reads the ID token only and never calls an API
              with the access token, so DPoP adds nothing here and would break the token request.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Register emisar's redirect URI on that app.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              The form shows the exact value, with a copy button — it's
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso/callback</code>
              on your emisar host. This is the fixed URI emisar uses; it's never taken from the
              request, so paste it verbatim into your app's allowed redirect / callback URLs.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Fill in the connection and save.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">Back on the emisar form, set:</p>
            <ul class="mt-2 space-y-2">
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Provider type</strong>
                  and a <strong class="text-zinc-200">Display name</strong>
                  (what your members see — e.g. "Acme Okta").
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Issuer URL</strong>
                  — your provider's OIDC issuer (must be HTTPS). emisar fetches the discovery
                  document from there, so you never paste individual endpoints. The in-app guide
                  shows the shape for the selected provider (your Okta org URL, your Keycloak
                  realm URL, and so on).
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client ID</strong>
                  and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client secret</strong>
                  from the app you just created. The secret is write-only — stored server-side,
                  redacted in the UI, and never rendered back (a sign-in secret has to be replayed to
                  your IdP, so it can't be hashed); at-rest protection is infrastructure-level. When you
                  edit the connection later, leave it blank to keep the current one.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">New-user provisioning</strong>
                  — <span class="text-zinc-300">Auto-provision</span>
                  creates a user on first sign-in; <span class="text-zinc-300">Manual</span>
                  holds each first-time sign-in as a pending request an admin approves. Either way
                  they land at the default role below.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Identifier claim</strong>
                  — leave as <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>;
                  Microsoft Entra can use
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">oid</code>
                  instead. emisar accepts only those two, not an arbitrary claim. This is what an
                  identity is bound to (see below), and what SCIM's
                  <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">externalId</code>
                  must match for login and directory sync to converge.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Default role for new users</strong>
                  — the role a newly provisioned member lands at (viewer / operator / billing manager / admin). Sync
                  never grants <span class="text-zinc-300">owner</span>; that stays a deliberate
                  human grant. See <.link
                    navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"}
                    class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
                  >roles</.link>.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  <strong class="text-zinc-200">Allowed email domain</strong>
                  (optional) — restricts sign-in to verified addresses on that domain and routes
                  that domain's sign-ins to this connection. Leave blank to accept any address the
                  provider asserts.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  Click <strong class="text-zinc-200">Test connection</strong>
                  before you save. emisar runs a live OIDC discovery round-trip against the issuer
                  and shows the authorization, token, and JWKS endpoints it found — so a wrong issuer
                  or an unreachable provider surfaces here, not at your team's first sign-in.
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
                <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
                <span>
                  Decide whether <strong class="text-zinc-200">this provider satisfies your 2FA
                  requirement</strong>
                  (see below), tick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enabled</strong>, and save. The
                  connection appears in the list, ready to sign in through.
                </span>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="okta">Okta setup</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Create one OIDC web app in Okta, then connect it to emisar. You need an Okta administrator
        and an emisar owner or admin. Keep
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team → Single sign-on → Add provider</strong>
        open so you can copy emisar's callback URL.
      </p>

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Create an OIDC web application.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              In the Okta Admin Console go to <strong class="text-zinc-200">Applications → Applications → Create App Integration</strong>. Choose
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">OIDC - OpenID Connect</strong>
              as the sign-in method, then <strong class="text-zinc-200">Web Application</strong>
              as the application type — that's the confidential client emisar needs, because it
              authenticates with a client secret.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-oidc-create.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · Create a new app integration"
              caption="Pick OIDC, and the Application type choices appear below it — emisar is a Web Application."
              alt="Okta's Create a new app integration dialog with OIDC - OpenID Connect selected among the sign-in methods, which reveals an Application type section below where Web Application is selected rather than Single-Page or Native Application."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Register emisar's callback as the only redirect.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Name the app, then paste emisar's callback into
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Sign-in redirect URIs</strong>
              — it's
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso/callback</code>
              on your emisar host, and emisar shows it with a copy button on the Add provider page.
              Make it the only entry and leave
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Allow wildcard *</strong>
              off; a wildcard redirect is an open-redirect risk. Leave
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Authorization Code</strong>
              ticked. For assignment,
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Skip group assignment for now</strong>
              is the right default — emisar decides who gets what through its own group&nbsp;→&nbsp;role
              mapping.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-oidc-settings.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · New Web App Integration"
              caption="One sign-in redirect URI, wildcard off, Authorization Code. Clear the sign-out box — emisar doesn't use it."
              alt="Okta's New Web App Integration form: app integration name emisar, Authorization Code ticked under Core grants, https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback as the only sign-in redirect URI, the allow-wildcard box unchecked, and an empty sign-out redirect URI field."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Copy the client credentials.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Saving lands you on the app's General tab with its
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client ID</strong>
              and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client secret</strong>. You need both, plus your <strong class="text-zinc-200">issuer</strong>.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/okta-oidc-credentials.webp"
              title="Okta Admin Console · Client Credentials"
              caption="Keep Client authentication on Client secret — emisar is a confidential client and replays that secret to your IdP."
              alt="The Okta app's General tab showing Client Credentials: the Client ID with a copy button, Client authentication set to Client secret rather than public key, PKCE unchecked, and a masked client secret with its creation date and Active status."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            4
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Connect it in emisar.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Back in emisar, pick the <strong class="text-zinc-200">Okta</strong>
              provider type and paste the client ID and secret. For the issuer, use your
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">org</strong>
              authorization server — that's your Okta org URL itself
              (<code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">https://your-org.okta.com</code>,
              no <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/oauth2/…</code>
              path). Leave
              the identifier claim at <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>. Tick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enabled</strong>, save, and sign in.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_callout kind={:warn} title="Use the org authorization server, not a custom one.">
        <p>
          A <strong class="text-zinc-200">custom</strong>
          authorization server lets an Okta admin rewrite the subject claim, and its tokens can carry
          an email as <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
          instead of the Okta user id. The org server keeps
          <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
          equal to the Okta user id — which is exactly what
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/scim"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">directory sync</.link>
          provisions on, so login and provisioning converge on one identity instead of creating two.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <p class="mt-8 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Next:</strong>
        add
        <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim#okta"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >directory sync</.link>
        so roles and removals follow Okta. It rides a second Okta application — an OIDC login app
        cannot carry SCIM.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="jumpcloud">JumpCloud setup</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Verified against a live JumpCloud tenant. You need a JumpCloud administrator and an emisar
        owner or admin, with
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team → Single sign-on → Add provider</strong>
        open in another tab.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="One application, both jobs">
        <p>
          JumpCloud is the only provider here whose custom application carries sign-in
          <em>and</em>
          directory sync together. If you want both, tick them on the same wizard step — you do not
          need a second connector.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Open SSO Applications.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              In the JumpCloud Admin Portal go to
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Access → SSO Applications</strong>
              and press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Add New Application</strong>.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-add-app.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · SSO Applications"
              caption="Every application JumpCloud signs people into is listed here. Provisioning shows against the ones that also push a directory."
              alt="The JumpCloud Admin Portal's SSO Applications page listing two configured applications, DEMO - RSA and emisar, the latter tagged Provisioning under Supported Functionality, with the Add New Application button at the top left outlined in green."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Choose Custom Application.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              emisar is not in JumpCloud's catalog, so scroll past the featured tiles to <strong class="text-zinc-200">Custom Application</strong>, press its <strong class="text-zinc-200">Select</strong>, then <strong class="text-zinc-200">Next</strong>.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-custom-app.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · Select Application"
              caption="The Custom Application tile sits last, after the catalog's featured integrations."
              alt="JumpCloud's Create New Application Integration wizard on step 1, Select Application, showing featured tiles for Google Workspace, AWS IAM Identity Center, Slack, Atlassian Cloud, Personio, CrowdStrike and Salesforce, with the Custom Application tile in the bottom-right corner outlined in green."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Enable SSO, and pick OIDC.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Tick <strong class="text-zinc-200">Manage Single Sign-On (SSO)</strong>. That reveals a
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Select One</strong>
              pair beneath it — choose <strong class="text-zinc-200">Configure SSO with OIDC</strong>.
              It defaults to SAML, and emisar's connection is OIDC, so this is the one click on the
              page that is easy to miss and hard to undo later.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4">
              If you also want directory sync, tick
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Export users to this app (Provisioning)</strong>
              on this same step — JumpCloud carries both on one application. Leave
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Import users from this app</strong>
              off; that direction pulls into JumpCloud.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-options.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · Select Options"
              caption="SSO with OIDC and Export users, together on one application. Import users stays off — it points the wrong way."
              alt="JumpCloud's Select Options step with Manage Single Sign-On (SSO) checked and outlined in green, its Select One radio pair showing Configure SSO with OIDC selected rather than the default SAML; Export users to this app (Provisioning) is also checked, Import users from this app and Add a bookmark are unchecked, and the Next button is enabled."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            4
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Name it.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Display Label</strong>
              is what your people see in the JumpCloud User Portal —
              <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar</code>
              is the obvious choice. Everything else on this step is cosmetic.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-general-info.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · Enter General Info"
              caption="Only Display Label is required."
              alt="JumpCloud's Enter General Info step with the Display Label field filled in with emisar and focused, an empty optional Description, a Color Indicator picker set to blue, and Show this application in User Portal checked."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            5
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Check the summary, then configure.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              The review step lists what you just chose under <strong class="text-zinc-200">Enabled Features</strong>. It should read
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">SSO with OIDC</strong>
              — and <strong class="text-zinc-200">User Export - Provisioning</strong>
              if you ticked it. If it says SAML, go <strong class="text-zinc-200">Back</strong>
              and change the radio; emisar cannot use a SAML connector. Then press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Configure Application</strong>.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-review.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · Review"
              caption="Enabled Features is the check that the OIDC radio actually took."
              alt="JumpCloud's Review step for an application about to be added, whose Application Summary panel shows Display Label emisar and Enabled Features listing both SSO with OIDC and User Export - Provisioning, outlined in green, beside a Configure Application button."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            6
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">
              Set the redirect URI, then swap credentials with emisar.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              On the application's <strong class="text-zinc-200">SSO</strong>
              tab, put emisar's callback in <strong class="text-zinc-200">Redirect URIs</strong>:
            </p>
            <.docs_code
              phx-no-format
              label="Redirect URI"
              copy_text="https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback"
            >https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback</.docs_code>
            <p class="mt-4">
              Leave <strong class="text-zinc-200">Authorization Code</strong>
              as the grant type. Either <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client Secret POST</strong>
              or <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client Secret Basic</strong>
              works — emisar accepts both. Set <strong class="text-zinc-200">Login URL</strong>
              to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">https://emisar.dev</code>, then press <strong class="text-zinc-200">Activate</strong>.
            </p>
            <p class="mt-4">
              Activating reveals the <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client ID</strong>
              and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client Secret</strong>. Those are the only two
              values you carry back: pick <strong class="text-zinc-200">JumpCloud</strong>
              in emisar's <strong class="text-zinc-200">Add provider</strong>
              form and the issuer is already filled in and locked, because it is the same for every
              JumpCloud org.
            </p>

            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/jumpcloud-sso-oidc-config.webp"
              title="JumpCloud · OIDC Single Sign-On Configuration"
              caption="Redirect URIs takes emisar's callback verbatim. The Provisioning tab beside SSO is where directory sync is wired, on this same application."
              alt="The SSO tab of a JumpCloud OpenID Connect application, with badges reading Single Sign-On Active, Client Secret Valid and Provisioning Inactive, and tabs for General Info, SSO, Provisioning, User Groups and Users. Under Configuration Settings the Authorization Code grant type is checked, the Redirect URIs label is outlined in green above a field containing https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback, Client Authentication Type is set to Client Secret POST, and an Activate button sits at the bottom right."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p class="mt-8 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Next:</strong>
        if you ticked <strong class="text-zinc-200">Export users to this app</strong>
        back on step 3, this same application is ready to push your directory — <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim#jumpcloud"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >finish directory sync</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="keycloak">Keycloak setup</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Create one confidential Keycloak client, then connect it to emisar. You need Keycloak
        realm-admin access and an emisar owner or admin. Keep
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Team → Single sign-on → Add provider</strong>
        open in emisar so you can copy its callback URL.
      </p>

      <.docs_callout kind={:note} title="OIDC login only">
        <p>
          This setup covers sign-in and just-in-time user provisioning. It does not configure
          Keycloak as a SCIM directory client. For supported directory provisioning, start with <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/scim"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >
            directory sync
          </.link>.
        </p>
      </.docs_callout>

      <ol class="mt-8 space-y-10">
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            1
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Start an OpenID Connect client.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              In the Keycloak Admin Console, choose your realm, then go to <strong class="text-zinc-200">Clients → Create client</strong>. Leave
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client type</strong>
              on <span class="text-zinc-300">OpenID Connect</span>, set
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client ID</strong>
              to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">emisar-portal</code>,
              and click <strong class="text-zinc-200">Next</strong>.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/keycloak-client-general.webp"
              title="Keycloak Admin Console · General settings"
              caption="Create an OpenID Connect client for the emisar web application."
              alt="Keycloak's Create client General settings step with OpenID Connect selected, emisar-portal as the client ID, and emisar as the name."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            2
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Enable only the flow emisar uses.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Turn <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client authentication</strong>
              and <strong class="text-zinc-200">Require PKCE</strong>
              on. Keep <strong class="text-zinc-200">Standard flow</strong>
              on and leave the PKCE method on <span class="text-zinc-300">S256</span>. Leave
              Authorization, DPoP, Direct access grants, Implicit flow, Service accounts roles,
              and every device flow off. Then click <strong class="text-zinc-200">Next</strong>.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/keycloak-client-capabilities.webp"
              title="Keycloak Admin Console · Capability config"
              caption="A confidential client using the authorization-code flow with PKCE."
              alt="Keycloak's Capability config step with Client authentication, Standard flow, and Require PKCE enabled; all other capabilities and authentication flows are disabled."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            3
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Register the exact redirect URI.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Copy the callback shown in emisar and paste it into <strong class="text-zinc-200">Valid redirect URIs</strong>. It ends in <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso/callback</code>.
              Do not add a wildcard. The other URL fields can stay empty; click <strong class="text-zinc-200">Save</strong>.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/keycloak-client-redirect.webp"
              title="Keycloak Admin Console · Login settings"
              caption="The screenshot uses a reserved example host; paste the callback from your emisar form verbatim."
              alt="Keycloak's Login settings step with https://emisar.dev/sign_in/sso/callback entered as the sole Valid redirect URI and no wildcard."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            4
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Copy the client secret.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Open the saved client's <strong class="text-zinc-200">Credentials</strong>
              tab. Copy its client secret into emisar's
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client secret</strong>
              field. Treat the value like a password; do not put it in tickets, chat, or
              screenshots.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/keycloak-client-secret.webp"
              title="Keycloak Admin Console · Credentials"
              caption="The client secret stays masked in this guide."
              alt="The Keycloak Credentials tab for emisar-portal with Client Id and Secret selected and the client secret hidden behind password dots."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            5
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Verify the identity scopes.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              On the <strong class="text-zinc-200">Client scopes</strong>
              tab, confirm that <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">email</code>
              and <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">profile</code>
              are assigned as <span class="text-zinc-300">Default</span>.
              emisar requests <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">openid</code>
              in the authorization request, so it does not appear as an assigned client-scope row.
            </p>
            <.docs_screenshot
              src="/images/docs/sso/keycloak-client-scopes.webp"
              title="Keycloak Admin Console · Client scopes"
              caption="The default email and profile scopes give emisar the verified identity claims it needs."
              alt="The Keycloak Client scopes table for emisar-portal with email and profile assigned as Default scopes."
            />
          </div>
        </li>
        <li class="flex gap-4">
          <span class="grid h-8 w-8 flex-none place-items-center rounded-full bg-brand-500/20 text-sm font-semibold text-brand-300">
            6
          </span>
          <div class="min-w-0 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            <p class="font-semibold text-zinc-100">Connect and test the first sign-in.</p>
            <p class="mt-1.5">
              Back in emisar, select the <strong class="text-zinc-200">Keycloak</strong>
              preset and enter the issuer URL <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">
                https://YOUR-KEYCLOAK-HOST/realms/YOUR-REALM
              </code>,
              client ID, and client secret. Choose
              <strong class="text-zinc-200">Auto-provision</strong>
              or <strong class="text-zinc-200">Manual</strong>
              new-user provisioning and a default role, test the connection, enable it, and save.
              In a private browser window, sign in as a Keycloak user who is not yet in emisar.
              Auto-provision should create the user at the default role; Manual should create a
              pending request for an admin instead.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h3 id="signing-in">How members sign in</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        On the sign-in page, members click <strong class="text-zinc-200">"Continue with single sign-on"</strong>, which sends them to <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">/sign_in/sso</code>; there they enter their team's address to reach that team's sign-in page, pick your provider, authenticate at the IdP, and come back signed in. An <strong class="text-zinc-200">allowed email domain</strong>, if set, is a guardrail on the return trip: a login whose IdP-verified domain doesn't match is refused.
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="subject-not-email">
        Identities are matched by the IdP's subject, never by email
      </.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar binds an SSO login to the provider plus the IdP's stable subject identifier (the
        <code class="rounded bg-zinc-900 px-1 py-0.5 text-xs">sub</code>
        claim, or whichever <strong class="text-zinc-200">identifier claim</strong>
        your provider uses) — <strong class="text-zinc-200">never by email address</strong>.
        Email-matching an SSO login onto an existing account is a known account-takeover vector, so
        we don't do it. The practical consequence: a just-in-time provisioned SSO user is a
        distinct principal, not a merge into a pre-existing account that happens to share
        an email. If an SSO email collides with an existing user, sign-in is refused rather than
        silently linked — an admin links the identity deliberately.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">First login provisions the user</strong>
        at the connection's default role (with the directory as the email authority).
      </p>

      <.docs_h3 id="sso-mfa">SSO sessions and your MFA requirement</.docs_h3>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each connection has a per-provider toggle — <em>"sign-in through this provider satisfies
        the account's 2FA requirement."</em>
        When it's on, an SSO session satisfies an account that
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access"} class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200">
          requires MFA
        </.link>
        without a second emisar TOTP prompt — your IdP enforces the MFA. Turn it off if you'd
        rather emisar still challenge. Magic-link sign-in is unaffected and is challenged for 2FA
        as usual.
      </p>

      <p class="mt-8 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Next:</strong>
        Keycloak cannot push a directory into emisar, so members are created on first sign-in and
        removed by hand — <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/scim#keycloak"}
          class="text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/40 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >what that leaves you to do</.link>.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="security-notes">Security notes</.docs_h2>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            Every SSO sign-in lands in the
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
              class="text-brand-300 hover:text-brand-200"
            >
              audit log
            </.link>
            with its authentication method, so you can answer "who did this, signed in how".
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
